Description: aic8800_fdrv (USB): reset the device when the firmware stops answering a command
 A command the firmware does not answer within RWNX_80211_CMD_TIMEOUT_MS marks the command manager
 RWNX_CMD_MGR_STATE_CRASHED, and nothing clears that but loading the driver again: every later command
 fails at once with -EPIPE ("cmd queue crashed"), so one late answer takes the radio off the network
 until someone reloads the driver or reboots.
 .
 Instead, ask the USB core to reset the device (usb_queue_reset_device): the driver is unbound, the port
 reset, the driver bound again and the firmware loaded afresh. On an AIC8800DC (a69c:88dc) the station
 interface is back and reaching its router 5 to 9 seconds after the timeout. At most once a minute and
 three times in ten minutes; a chip that keeps failing is left crashed, for whatever watches the system.
 .
 Module parameters: recover_on_timeout (default 1; 0 keeps the old behaviour) and, for testing,
 fake_cmd_timeout (writable at run time: the next N commands count as timed out), so the recovery can
 be tried without waiting for a real firmware stall; and, read-only, recoveries (how many resets were
 asked for) and recovered_at (when the last was, in seconds since boot, as /proc/uptime), so whatever
 watches the system can leave the radio to the driver while it comes back, without depending on the
 kernel log line, which a busy log can overwrite within seconds.
 .
 Tested on a Luckfox Lyra Zero W (RK3506, AIC8800DC on USB): a real stall, provoked by moving the
 station between access points on three channels every 30 s while the AP interface served a client
 (an SM_DISCONNECT_REQ timed out), was reset by the driver and the station back on its network in 54 s.
 .
 Interfaces the driver does not create again by itself (a hotspot's AP interface, say) have to be
 brought back from user space after the reset.
